# =============================================================================
# Drift monitoring -- CORRECTED 2026-07-13 (roadmap 6.19). READ THIS BEFORE EDITING.
# =============================================================================
# This block used to read:
#
#   # NOTE: alibi-detect dropped -- ... Functionality replaced by
#   # scipy.stats (KS, chi2), nannyml (CBPE), river (online drift), evidently
#   # (tabular distribution drift).
#   evidently==0.7.6
#   nannyml==0.13.0
#   great-expectations>=1.0,<2
#   fairlearn==0.12.0
#   defusedxml
#   beautifulsoup4
#
# EVERY CLAIM IN THAT COMMENT WAS AUDITED ON 2026-07-13. Two of the four were false:
#
#   scipy.stats (PSI / Kolmogorov-Smirnov / Maximum Mean Discrepancy)  TRUE  -- monitoring/drift_detector.py
#   river (online drift)                                               TRUE  -- agent_layer/agents/annotation_policy_agent.py
#   nannyml (Confidence-Based Performance Estimation)                  FALSE -- NO CODE CALLED IT.
#   evidently (tabular distribution drift)                             FALSE -- called through an API DELETED two major versions ago.
#
# * nannyml was imported by exactly ONE file: scripts/verify_drift_libs.py, which printed its
#   version number. There was NO Confidence-Based Performance Estimation implementation
#   anywhere in this codebase. The capability was claimed, never built.
# * evidently was called via `evidently.report` / `evidently.metric_preset` /
#   `evidently.pipeline.column_mapping` -- ALL THREE DELETED. run_drift_monitor.py caught the
#   ImportError and logged "Evidently AI not installed. Run: pip install evidently", which was
#   FALSE -- it WAS installed. The code misdiagnosed its own failure and the drift report was
#   silently never produced, for months.
# * great-expectations and fairlearn were imported by NOTHING except verify_drift_libs.py.
#   The fairness agent (agent_layer/agents/fairness_subgroup_agent.py) does not use fairlearn.
# * defusedxml and beautifulsoup4 were imported NOWHERE at all.
#
# And NONE of them were in requirements.txt -- the ONLY file Continuous Integration, Docker and
# every launch script install -- so none of them were ever installed on a clean machine. They
# existed solely in the developer's virtual environment, where they were the sole cause of all
# SIX `pip check` conflicts (evidently->plotly<6; nannyml->lightgbm<4.6, plotly<6, Jinja2<3.1;
# plus gcsfs/s3fs, which nannyml dragged in). Removing them made `pip check` clean: 6 -> 0.
#
# WHAT LIVES WHERE NOW
# --------------------
#   TRAINING (this file -> requirements.txt): pandera, river, scipy, pyspark.
#     These are the drift components that are ACTUALLY WIRED and that ACTUALLY WORK.
#
#   DRIFT (requirements-drift.txt, a SEPARATE environment): nannyml, evidently.
#     They cannot live here. nannyml requires lightgbm>=3.3,<4.6 (verified in BOTH 0.13.0 and
#     0.13.1 METADATA) while the ensemble TRAINS on lightgbm 4.6.0 -- a BASE MODEL. Evidently
#     requires plotly<6 while this project runs plotly 6.6.0. Downgrading a base model of the
#     classifier to satisfy a MONITORING library would change the science to suit the
#     instrument. That trade is refused. See roadmap 6.19.
#
# Confidence-Based Performance Estimation is now REAL, in the isolated environment:
# src/genomic_variant_classifier/monitoring/performance_estimator.py, wired into
# scripts/run_drift_monitor.py --estimate-performance, and tested in tests_drift/.
# Verified 2026-07-13: it estimates ROC AUC on data with the labels removed.
#
# See docs/hypotheses/HYP_alibi-detect-numpy2-lag.md for the alibi-detect decision.
